   :db/valueType :db.type/ref
   :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many
   :db/doc "RDFS-style sub-property declaration — `(p :dt/subproperty-of q)` iff `IEXT(p) ⊆ IEXT(q)`.  Reflexive + transitive per Hayes & Patel-Schneider 2014 §5.2.  Operational consequence (RDFS rule rdfs7): `(p :dt/subproperty-of q) ∧ (x p y) → (x q y)` is entailed.  Used to declare predicate hierarchy: e.g., `(:mm.memory/evidences :dt/subproperty-of :mm.memory/cites)` means every evidences-edge is also a cites-edge.  Per `observations/predicate_hierarchy_lattice_analysis_and_substrate_memorial_layered_duplication_2026_05_21.md`."}

  {:db/ident :dt/inverse-of
   :dt/type  :dt/Property
   :dt/domain :dt/Property
   :dt/range :dt/Property
   :db/valueType :db.type/ref
   :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one
   :db/doc "OWL 2 owl:inverseOf alignment.  Declares two predicates as inverses: `(p :dt/inverse-of q)` iff `(x p y) ↔ (y q x)`.  Symmetric in semantics (if p inverse-of q then q inverse-of p) — but cardinality-one here records the directional assertion; the symmetric companion is asserted separately if needed.  Operational consequences (OWL 2 RL rules prp-inv1 + prp-inv2): `(p :dt/inverse-of q) ∧ (x p y) → (y q x)` AND `(p :dt/inverse-of q) ∧ (x q y) → (y p x)`.  Substrate-level encoding of the inverse-of relation that lives informally in predicate-memorial frontmatter (`inverse-of:` field).  Layer 0 entailment rules per `references/rdfs_entailment_rules_datalog_compilation_reference.md` §4.6 + `plans/layered_rule_subsystem_architecture_arc_2026_05_21.md`."}

